4; the 1984 benchmark to December 1989; and the 1989 benchmark to date. Includes savings and loan associations, credit unions, nonprofit institutions, mutual savings banks, corporate pension trust funds, dealers and brokers, certain Government deposit accounts, and Government-sponsored enterprises.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 85, 173, 116], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"52\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [703, 85, 929, 113], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"MARKET YIELDS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [543, 196, 1087, 231], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Market Yields\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 285, 794, 398], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"The tables and charts in this section present yields on Treasury marketable securities, and compare long-term yields on Treasury securities with yields on long-term corporate and municipal securities.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 398, 794, 549], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"Table MY-1 lists Treasury market bid yields at constant maturities for bills, notes, and bonds. The Treasury yield curve in the accompanying chart, is based on current market bid quotations on the most actively traded Treasury securities as of 3:30 p.m. on the last business day of the calendar quarter.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 559, 794, 721], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"Treasury obtains quotations from the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, which compiles quotations provided by five primary dealers. Treasury uses these composite quotations to derive the yield curve, based on semiannual interest payments and read at constant maturity points to develop a consistent data series. Yields on Treasury bills are coupon\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 285, 1490, 394], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"equivalent yields of bank discount rates at which Treasury bills trade in the market. The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System publishes the Treasury constant maturity data series in its weekly H.15 press release.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 396, 1490, 721], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"Table MY-2 shows average yields of long-term Treasury, corporate, and municipal bonds. The long-term Treasury average yield is the 30-year constant maturity yield. The corporate bond average yield is developed by Treasury by calculating reoffering yields on new long-term securities maturing in at least 20 years and rated Aa by Moody's Investors Service. The municipal bond average yield prior to 1991 was compiled by Treasury. Beginning with January 1991, the average yield is the \\\"Municipal Bond Yield Average,\\\" published by Moody's Investors Service for 20-year reoffering yields on selected Aa-rated general obligations. See the footnotes for further explanation.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [171, 863, 1459, 896], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"TABLE MY-1.\\u2014Treasury Market Bid Yields at Constant Maturities: Bills, Notes, and Bonds*\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [636, 926, 996, 948], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"[In percentages. Source: Office of Market Finance]\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 943, 1492, 1874], \"page_id\": 54}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th>Date</th><th>3-mo. (1)</th><th>6-mo. (2)</th><th>1-yr. (3)</th><th>2-yr. (4)</th><th>3-yr. (5)</th><th>5-yr. (6)</th><th>7-yr. (7)</th><th>10-yr. (8)</th><th>30-yr. (9)</th></tr><tr>