, \"content\": \"U.S. banking claims on foreigners, excluding claims represented by long-term securities, are highly concentrated in international financial centers. The chart and table show that well more than half of claims are reported opposite the United Kingdom, Japan, and, most notably, offshore banking centers in the Caribbean. Also significant are claims on foreigners reported against Europe excluding the United Kingdom, and Asia excluding Japan.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [239, 1761, 794, 1853], \"page_id\": 82}], \"content\": \"Total U.S. banking claims on foreigners declined in 1992, recovered modestly over the next 2 years, and then surged $59 billion to a new high at\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [838, 1502, 1386, 1817], \"page_id\": 82}], \"content\": \"the end of 1995. Compared with yearend 1994, claims on each area shown expanded in 1995 (except for the group \\\"All other countries,\\\" against which claims declined marginally) reaching new highs for all but Japan. The largest increase in U.S. claims occurred opposite the Caribbean banking centers, which expanded by $22.2 billion to $229.9 billion, the highest level since 1991. Increases in U.S. claims opposite other Europe and other Asia were also substantial at $17.5 billion and $8.8 billion, respectively.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [668, 90, 962, 113], \"page_id\": 83}], \"content\": \"CAPITAL MOVEMENTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1455, 90, 1484, 113], \"page_id\": 83}], \"content\": \"81\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [364, 193, 1264, 306], \"page_id\": 83}], \"content\": \"SECTION IV.\\u2014Liabilities to, and Claims on, Foreigners Reported by Nonbanking Business Enterprises in the United States\", \"description\": nu